ORIGINAL WORK
=============

Galactic Empire was written by Michael B. Murdock for The Major BBS and
released as a commercial module by M.B. Murdock & Associates. The version this
project works from is release 3.2e, dated 1994-08-06.

    Copyright (C) 1988, 1989, 1990, 1991, 1992 Michael B. Murdock

Murdock later released the source publicly under the GNU General Public
License, version 2 or (at the author's option clause) any later version. Every
C file in the distribution carries that notice, along with his own note:

    "The source code to this program is being made available to the general
     public in the hopes that it will continue to live on and evolve."

This port is that continuation. It is a reimplementation in TypeScript, not a
recompilation, but it was written by reading Murdock's C source and it embeds
his data verbatim, so it is offered under a compatible copyleft license. The
AGPL was chosen over the GPL because this port is played over a network, which
is exactly the case the GPL does not cover.


WHAT IS INCLUDED FROM THE ORIGINAL
==================================

  reference/                     The original distribution, vendored unmodified
                                 for reference. Read only. Not compiled, not
                                 executed, not shipped in any built image.

  backend/src/game/commands/canon-messages.generated.ts
                                 1,070 message strings taken verbatim from
                                 GE/REL/MBMGEMSG.MSG, ANSI colour stripped.
                                 Generated by tools/extract-messages.mjs.

  backend/prisma/seed/ship-classes.ts
                                 The 34-class ship table, extracted field by
                                 field from GE/REL/MBMGESHP.MSG. Generated by
                                 tools/extract-ship-classes.mjs.

  backend/src/game/commands/help/canon-help.generated.ts
                                 61 in-game help entries, verbatim from
                                 GE/REL/MBMGEHLP.MSG.

  backend/src/game/commands/help/class-help.generated.ts
                                 The 34 per-class specification pages, verbatim
                                 from GE/REL/MBMGESHP.MSG.

  backend/src/game/cybertron/cyb-taunt-catalog.generated.ts
  backend/src/game/droid/droid-annoy-catalog.generated.ts
                                 The Cybertron and Droid taunt catalogues, from
                                 GE/REL/MBMGEMSG.MSG.

  backend/src/game/galaxy/s00.ts
                                 The neutral-zone planet fixture, from the
                                 shipped sysop option database.

Each of those seven files carries Murdock's copyright in its own header, and
the tools/extract-*.mjs scripts emit that header, so regenerating cannot strip
it. Any new generator must do the same.

Game balance constants throughout backend/src carry @see citations to the C
file and line they came from. Those citations are load-bearing, not decoration:
they are how a reader tells canon from our own work.


HOW THIS PORT WAS WRITTEN
=========================

This port was written by Rick Earley in collaboration with Claude, Anthropic's
AI coding assistant, using Claude Code. The commit history records it directly:
hundreds of the commits in this repository carry a `Co-Authored-By: Claude`
trailer.

It is stated here because the method is load-bearing rather than incidental. A
faithful port is an exercise in reading sixteen thousand lines of 1990s C and
not getting a number wrong, and this project is built around not trusting any
single reading of that source -- human or otherwise:

  - Every gameplay constant carries an @see citation to the C file and line it
    was taken from. A test re-reads the original and fails if a citation quotes
    a line that is not there.

  - Canon data is generated from the shipped .MSG files, never hand-typed. That
    rule exists because hand-transcription put wrong numbers into this codebase
    twice before it did.

  - Where the port knowingly differs from the original, it is a numbered entry
    in docs/DECISIONS.md, tagged in the code, and checked in both directions by
    the test suite. A deviation that is not written down is a bug.

  - Behaviour is pinned by over six thousand automated backend tests, and the
    balance layer among them re-reads the original distribution rather than
    asserting a remembered value.

None of that makes the port correct. It makes an error findable, which is the
most any reimplementation of a game this old can honestly promise. Where you
find one, the original is right and this is wrong. See docs/DECISIONS.md for
how such a finding gets recorded.


ATTRIBUTION AND CORRECTIONS
===========================

The vendored copy of the distribution was obtained from
https://github.com/bsimser/ge, retrieved 2026-09-02, republished by Bil Simser
in 2018. That repository adds an MIT LICENSE file at its root. The per-file GPL
headers written by Murdock govern the code itself; a republisher cannot
relicense an upstream author's work by adding a file beside it. This project
therefore treats the inbound terms as GPL-2.0-or-later, not MIT.

Elwynor Technologies maintain Galactic Empire today. They state they arranged
to take over the M.B. Murdock & Associates products, and they released their
own port of the game to 32-bit Worldgroup and The Major BBS V10 publicly in
2021, at https://github.com/elwynor/elwge, under AGPL-3.0. They are
acknowledged here as the game's current stewards.

  No code, data, or fix from Elwynor's port is used in this project.

Both statements are meant to stand together. What is vendored here is Murdock's
original DOS-era C source, used under the GPL grant he wrote into its headers —
a grant that runs with the code and is not affected by who holds the copyright
today. Where the community wiki lists fixes Elwynor made to their own port,
those are recorded as external observations and were not copied.

Two other ports of this game are worth naming, because both descend from the
same republished copy this one does and neither is used here:

  https://github.com/elwynor/elwge     Elwynor Technologies, AGPL-3.0
  https://github.com/manicpop/ge-next  Anthony Schmidt / ManicPop.org, AGPL-3.0,
                                       a 2024 fork of bsimser/ge

The community wiki at reference/wiki/ is a third-party transcription, used for
orientation only. It has been caught contradicting the C source and is never
cited against it.


